.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1230 PM PST Sun Nov 8 2026
(This evening through Monday night)

High pressure remains the dominate weather pattern aloft leading
to our warming trend, despite a weakening frontal boundary to our
north. As such, temperatures will remain 5-10 deg F above early-
November climatological norms. Meanwhile, high clouds continue to
stream in from the west. Tonight and into Monday morning, there is
the potential for low clouds and/or fog in the Bay Area, much of
the Santa Clara Valley, East Bay Valleys, and North Bay Valleys.
Similar to today, any fog that does develop will likely dissipate
between 9 AM - 10 AM and give way to mostly sunny conditions by
the afternoon. Temperatures Monday afternoon will be within a few
degrees of those today.

Got beach plans? Check out the BEACHES section of the AFD for
safety tips and more information on the Beach Hazards Statement
and Coastal Flood Advisory.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1230 PM PST Sun Nov 8 2026
(Tuesday through next Saturday)

High pressure will continue to strengthen over the Bay Area and
Central Coast on Tuesday and into Wednesday. This will result in
temperatures across the interior Central Coast to be some 10-15 deg
F above normal with the northern portions of our region remaining 5-
10 deg F above. The high pressure is forecast to shift eastward into
the Desert Southwest on Thursday as the trough off of the West Coast
deepens.

Rain chances have largely been pushed back to Friday and Saturday
with both deterministic GFS and ECMWF showing a cut-off low pressure
system developing and pushing inland over Southern California or
Baja California from Friday through Sunday. However, there is still
chances for rain during this timeframe as the main trough axis
shifts inland across the Pacific Northwest and Northern California
Saturday through Monday. Be sure to check back for the latest
forecast information as we near late week and into the weekend.
Exact details on the system`s trajectory and expected rainfall
amounts will be refined.
